back uses the original numbers and method.","options":["44 cm; 38 cm; 27 cm.","Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Omitting units or skipping a side produces incomplete perimeters.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers. Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["The new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Solve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for yo\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant working with accurate reasoning and named places or values.","question":"Solve a fresh problem that uses the central skill. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Set: square 7 cm; rectangle 9 cm×6 cm; irregular 3+5+4+6 cm.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change value or method, not just make the working longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected working and a method-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely calculation or place-value error, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Omitting units or skipping a side produces incomplete perimeters. Correction: 44 cm; 38 cm; 27 cm.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w23-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise fluency.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use fluency with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify perimeter and explain why it matters.","I can practise sides independently on a new prompt.","I can notice unit needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Perimeter fluency adds side lengths accurately and includes units in every answer. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Perimeter fluency adds side lengths accurately and includes units in every answer.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSet: square 7 cm; rectangle 9 cm×6 cm; irregular 3+5+4+6 cm.\n\nTRY FIRST\nFind P: square 11 cm; rectangle 15 cm by 4 cm; triangle 8+9+10.\n\nFEEDBACK\n44 cm; 38 cm; 27 cm.","explain":"Perimeter fluency adds side lengths accurately and includes units in every answer.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Omitting units or skipping a side produces incomplete perimeters. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"P